title The industry word on our own noun
prompt Keaton -- can we just use flaky test if that is the industry standard, that is more comlink tendency straightforward, we can molt all working trees
think Keaton's argument is right on comlink-tendency's own test: the clearest, most fun, safest word wins at whatever length, and sometimes that word is the one the world already uses.
think I had recommended keeping flap twice, so I checked rather than defended. The tree twice rejected an industry standard -- red over bug, first resident over dogfooding -- yet BOTH replaced industry words that were POOR. Flaky test is a GOOD one, so the pattern does not reach it.
obs MY OWN COUNT WAS WRONG AND I CAUGHT IT BY CHECKING THE SENSE. I reported 253 uses of flaky already in the tree and nearly argued the tree had voted. 240 of those are NIX FLAKES -- flake.nix, the cloud pier flake -- and only 13 carry the test sense.
obs That mistake surfaced the one real cost, so it was worth making: flake is a load-bearing noun here at 240 uses, and flaky is its ordinary-English adjective. The collision is at grep level rather than comprehension level, and the rule names it rather than hiding it.
obs THE NOUN CHANGES, because this tree has no tests. It has guards, witnesses, scans and controls. So the industry ADJECTIVE rides our own NOUN: a flaky guard, never a flaky test.
obs FLAP WAS NOT ONLY PROSE. It was an emitted field -- flap=yes, flap=no, verdict=flap -- that a scan writes and a control reads back BY NAME. I renamed the emitter first and the control would have read a key that no longer existed, which the read-through caught before any run. Emitter and reader moved together.
obs My first measurement missed four .sh files entirely, because I grepped md, rish, rye and kyri alone. Widening to every extension took the count from 8 living files to 12 and found all three renamable paths.
obs LEFT THE LEDGER'S ROWS ALONE. A REDS row records what was found when it was found, and the ledger's first law is that a row is never edited, so the rows keep flap and the new rule page is where a reader learns the living word.
obs Named the cheap fact Keaton may not have: the eight ships share one repository, so ONE sweep pushed reaches all eight at their next round-open pull rather than eight sweeps.
loom molt living_files=12 renamed_paths=3 occurrences=30 remaining_living=0 modes_preserved=3
loom counts flake_nix_sense=240 flaky_test_sense=13 flap_before=122
loom grade page=vocabulary-flaky composite=89 letter=B+ setting=door
rota none -- a word molt run on Keaton's word, no rota this lap
file .claude/rules/vocabulary-flaky.md the rule, with the flake collision named and the molt measured
file .cursor/rules/vocabulary-flaky.mdc the canonical twin
file tools/m/mantra_query_wire_flakiness_witness.rish renamed from flap, GREEN on metal after the move
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri the roster repointed in the same commit
scope one word molted across twelve living files, three paths renamed, one emitted field moved with its reader, one rule seated in both editors
status GREEN on both mantra witnesses after the rename, ascii_document, prose_register, and the three pin guards
recommend check in (Keaton) -- flaky is seated; construction/archive/ at 975 entries and the seven gated reds are still open
